<decompilertest>
<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:32:default:windows">
<!--
    MSVC __ftol-family call-fixup (kuna_msvcftol).

    MSVC compiles an x86-32 float-to-integer conversion as `fld <src>; call
    __ftol`: the value arrives in the x87 stack top ST0 and the __int64 result
    returns in EDX:EAX. No vendored x86 prototype model has an <input> pentry
    naming an x87 register (ST0 appears only as an <output> pentry), so the call
    characterizes ST0 as NoContainment, Heritage never creates an argument
    trial, and with nothing reading ST0 the whole feeding FLD chain is
    dead-code eliminated together with every register it was based on.

    The XML wrapper only supplies the harness architecture. The script clears it
    and loads the real i386 COFF fixture, whose `ftol_conv` performs exactly the
    MSVC sequence twice against an UNDEFINED `__ftol` extern.

    Without the fixup the defect is two argument-less `__ftol()` calls, with no
    trace of the float loads that fed them (assert #1 pins that form to zero).
    The synthesized callfixup replaces each CALL with the helper's p-code. The fields are declared as integer words, so their
    bits are first reinterpreted as float before numeric truncation. The
    `(int4)(int8)` shape is deliberate: `_ftol`
    returns an __int64, and modelling it at 32 bits would silently produce the
    wrong high word for an `(__int64)d` conversion.

    Assertions span the one print.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
c3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="harness_stub"/>
</binaryimage>
<script>
  <com>clear architecture</com>
  <com>load file decompiler/crates/kuna-analysis/tests/fixtures/ftol_i386.obj</com>
  <com>read symbols</com>
  <com>load function ftol_conv</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>quit</com>
</script>
<stringmatch name="MSVCFTOL #1: no argument-less helper call survives" min="0" max="0">__ftol\(\)</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="MSVCFTOL #2: the first conversion casts the field it actually reads" min="1" max="1">union \{ unsigned int from; float to;.*a0\[6\]</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="MSVCFTOL #3: the second conversion casts the field it actually reads" min="1" max="1">union \{ unsigned int from; float to;.*a0\[7\]</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="MSVCFTOL #4: both conversions truncate at the helper's real 64-bit width" min="2" max="2">\(int4\)\(int8\)</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="MSVCFTOL #5: the call is replaced by an injection" min="1" max="1">// injected</stringmatch>
</decompilertest>
